;; Docs for std/test/prop.bjo, read through (:doc #:file "prop.bjodoc"). The
;; texts are samizdat markup: code in prose is @code{...}.

(:doc #:module
  (summary "Property tests: for-all runs a body with many generated values, shrinks a failing case, and stores it for the next run.")
  (see for-all assume classify label)
  (example """
    (import (std prelude))
    (import (std test))
    (import (std test gen))
    (import (std test prop))

    (deftest "reverse twice is the identity"
      (for-all ((xs : (List int)))
        (check (= (list-reverse (list-reverse xs)) xs))))

    (deftest "a die roll is in range"
      (for-all ((n (gen-int-range 1 6)))
        (def r (roll-with n))
        (check (>= r 1))
        (check (<= r 6))))

    (deftest "orderedmap-set then ref"
      (for-all #:runs 500 ((m : (OrderedMap string int)) (k : string) (v : int))
        (check (= (orderedmap-ref (orderedmap-set m k v) k) v))))

    (run-tests)
    """)
  (reference """
    @code{for-all} is a form in the body of a @code{deftest}. It runs its
    body many times, each time with new values from the generators of
    @code{(std test gen)}. The checks of @code{(std test)} in the body tell
    whether a run passes. When a run fails, @code{for-all} shrinks it to a
    small case, reports that case under the test, and stores it beside the
    test file. The next run of the test tries the stored case first.

    @strong{Runs.} A @code{for-all} runs its body 100 times, or
    @code{--runs n} times, or @code{#:runs n} times. Each run has a size,
    from 1 at the first run to 100 at the last, so the first runs try small
    values. A run fails when a check in it fails, when it raises an
    exception, or when it leaves a resource open. The body of each run runs
    in a scope of its own, inside the scope of the test. The scope waits for
    the fibers that the run started, so the next run does not share them.
    The test has @code{#:open-ok} to allow open resources.

    The first run that fails stops the random runs. A failed
    @code{for-all} does not stop its test, as a failed @code{check} does not.
    A test can have more than one @code{for-all}.

    @strong{The report.} A failure shows where the @code{for-all} is, which
    run failed and the seed of the run, the values of the bindings after
    shrinking, and the failures of the shrunk case:

    @codeblock[#:lang "text"]|{
    FAIL reverse is not the identity (list_test.bjo:6)
      for-all at list_test.bjo:7 failed on run 4 of 100 (seed 1234567890)
      shrunk 9 time(s) to:
        xs = (0 1)
      list_test.bjo:8: (check (= (list-reverse xs) xs))
        (list-reverse xs) => (1 0)
        xs                => (0 1)
    }|

    The second line says @code{with:} when shrinking found no smaller
    case. It says @code{shrunk n time(s), then stopped, out of time or
    replays, at:} when a limit stopped shrinking. When the generators raise
    an exception that is not a discard, the report shows the message and no
    values.

    @strong{Discarded runs.} @code{assume} discards a run, and so does a
    generator that gives up, such as @code{gen-such-that}. A discarded run
    is not counted. When more than 10 times the runs are discarded, the
    @code{for-all} fails, because the property was hardly tested.

    @strong{Shrinking.} Shrinking does not change the values. It changes the
    draws that the generators recorded, and replays them. Because a smaller
    draw gives a simpler value (see @code{(std test gen)}), a smaller vector
    of draws gives a simpler case. One round of shrinking does these steps,
    in this order:

    @itemlist{
      @item{Delete blocks of 8, then 4, 2 and 1 draws, at each place.}
      @item{Set blocks of 8, then 4, 2 and 1 draws to 0, at each place.}
      @item{Make each draw smaller: try 0, and else search by halving for
        the smallest value that fails between 0 and the draw.}
      @item{Swap adjacent draws that are out of order.}
      @item{Only when the steps above found nothing, two steps that cost
        more replays: delete a block of 2 or 1 draws and add their sum to a
        draw near it, and move all or half of a draw to a later draw near
        it. "Near" is at most 8 places away. These steps shrink a list
        whose elements must add up to a value.}
    }

    A change is kept when the replay fails, with any failure, and the draws
    that the replay used are smaller than the best so far. Fewer draws are
    smaller. Of two vectors of the same length, the one that is smaller at
    the first place where they differ is smaller. Every kept change makes
    the draws smaller, so shrinking ends. The rounds repeat until a round
    keeps nothing.

    Two limits stop shrinking early: @code{--shrink-limit n} replays, 2000
    when not given, and the deadline of the test. Shrinking stops when the
    time left is not more than a fifth of the time that was left when
    shrinking started. The report then shows the smallest case found.

    @strong{Flaky properties.} Before it shrinks, @code{for-all} replays the
    failed run. When the replay passes, the property depends on something
    other than its draws, such as the time, a shared state or the
    scheduler. The report then says that the case passed when it ran again,
    and shows the values and the failures without shrinking. Nothing is
    stored.

    @strong{Seeds.} Each @code{for-all} draws from a source made from the
    seed of its test and its index in the test. The seed of the test comes
    from the seed of the run and the name of the test (see
    @code{test-seed}). So choosing tests with filters, or adding a test,
    does not change what the other tests draw. A report shows the seed of
    the run. Run the test file again with @code{--seed} and that seed, and
    the name of the test as a filter, to repeat the run.

    @strong{The failure file.} A shrunk failure is stored in a file beside
    the test file: @code{list_test.bjo} stores to
    @code{list_test.failed.bjodat}. The file holds the draws, not the
    values, so no value has to be written as text:

    @codeblock[#:lang "bjolang"]|{
    (failed
      (case (test "reverse is not the identity") (index 0) (seed "1234567890") (size 4) (draws [1 0 1 1 0]))
      (case (test "parse round trip") (index 1) (seed "1234567890") (size 37) (draws [2 -1 0])))
    }|

    @itemlist{
      @item{@code{test} is the name of the test, and @code{index} the
        position of the @code{for-all} among the @code{for-all} forms that
        the test ran, from 0. A case is found by these two, not by a line
        number, because lines change when the file is edited.}
      @item{@code{seed} is the seed of the run that found the case, for a
        person to read.}
      @item{@code{size} is the size of the run. A replay uses it.}
      @item{@code{draws} are the shrunk draws. A draw above
        @code{long-max} is written as a ulong, @code{18446744073709551615UL},
        and the others as plain numbers.}
    }

    A @code{for-all} first replays each case that the file has for it.
    When a case fails, it is shrunk again and reported, its draws are
    updated, and the random runs do not run. The other cases of the
    @code{for-all} are then removed. When all the cases pass, they are
    removed, and the random runs run. A new shrunk failure is added at the
    end. A @code{for-all} keeps at most 10 cases.

    The file is read when a @code{for-all} first needs it. It is written at
    the end of @code{(run-tests)}, through @code{at-run-end!}, and only when
    its text changed. When it has no case left, it is deleted. A run
    without filters also removes the cases of tests that no longer exist.
    @code{run-test} alone does not write the file: the cases stay in
    memory, and a later run in the same program sees them. With
    @code{--no-store}, the file is not read or written.

    A file that does not read as cases is treated as empty. Whether to
    commit the file is the choice of the project. When it is committed, CI
    tries every case that anyone found.

    @strong{Statistics.} @code{classify} and @code{collect} count the runs
    that passed. With @code{--stats}, each @code{for-all} prints its counts
    when it ends:

    @codeblock[#:lang "text"]|{
    for-all at list_test.bjo:7: 100 run(s)
      23% empty
      4% long
    }|
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Properties
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc for-all
  (summary "Runs the body with many generated values. A failing case is shrunk, reported and stored.")
  (form (for-all ((name gen) ...) body ...))
  (form (for-all ((name : Type) ...) body ...))
  (form (for-all #:runs n ((name gen) ...) body ...))
  (arg name "The name that the body sees the value by. The report shows the value under this name.")
  (arg gen "A generator, a @code{(Gen T)}, that makes the value.")
  (arg Type "A type with an @code{Arbitrary} instance. Its @code{arbitrary} makes the value.")
  (arg n "The number of runs. When not given, @code{--runs}, and else 100.")
  (rest body "The forms of a run. At least one form. Their values are dropped.")
  (see assume classify collect label deftest)
  (example """
    (deftest "sort keeps the length"
      (for-all ((xs : (List int)))
        (check (= (list-length (list-sort xs)) (list-length xs)))))

    (deftest "a sum of small numbers"
      (for-all #:runs 1000 ((a (gen-int-range 0 100)) (b (gen-int-range 0 100)))
        (check (<= (+ a b) 200))))
    """)
  (reference """
    Write it in the body of a @code{deftest}. It finds its test when it
    runs. Outside a test it raises @code{System.InvalidOperationException}.

    The two kinds of bindings can be mixed in one @code{for-all}. All
    bindings draw in order, from the choices of the run. To make values
    that depend on each other, use @code{gen-let} in one binding.

    For each run, the bindings are drawn, and then the body runs in a scope
    of its own. A run passes when no check in it fails, it raises no
    exception, and it leaves no resource open. The failures of a run are not
    added to the test. Only the failures of the shrunk case are added. See
    the module for the runs, the report, shrinking and the failure file.

    The report shows each value with @code{->str}.
    """))

(:doc assume
  (summary "Discards the run when ok is #f. A discarded run is not counted.")
  (arg ok "The condition that the values of the run must satisfy.")
  (raises InvalidOperationException "When it is not in a run of a for-all.")
  (see for-all gen-such-that)
  (example """
    (for-all ((xs : (List int)))
      (assume (not (list-empty? xs)))
      (check (= (list-length (list-tail xs)) (- (list-length xs) 1))))
    """)
  (reference """
    It leaves the run with an exception. Do not catch
    @code{System.Exception} around it, or the run goes on.

    When more than 10 times the runs of a @code{for-all} are discarded, the
    @code{for-all} fails. A condition that most values do not satisfy tests
    little. Prefer a generator that makes good values directly.
    """))

(:doc classify
  (summary "Counts the run under name when ok is #t. --stats prints the counts.")
  (arg name "The name of the class.")
  (arg ok "Whether the run is in the class.")
  (see collect for-all)
  (example """
    (for-all ((xs : (List int)))
      (classify "empty" (list-empty? xs))
      (classify "long" (> (list-length xs) 10))
      (check (= (list-reverse (list-reverse xs)) xs)))
    """)
  (reference """
    Only the random runs that pass are counted, not the replays of shrinking
    or of stored cases. Outside a run, it does nothing.

    The counts show whether the generators make the values that the
    property is meant to test. A passing test prints nothing, so the counts
    are printed only with @code{--stats}, as a percentage of the runs, the
    largest first.
    """))

(:doc collect
  (summary "Counts the run under the value, as text. --stats prints the counts.")
  (arg value "The value. Runs with equal texts are counted together.")
  (see classify)
  (example """
    (for-all ((xs : (List int)))
      (collect (list-length xs))
      (check (= (list-reverse (list-reverse xs)) xs)))
    """)
  (reference """
    @code{(collect v)} is @code{(classify (->str v) #t)}.
    """))

(:doc label
  (summary "Runs the body, and reports each failure in it under the name.")
  (form (label name body ...))
  (arg name "A string: the name that the report puts above each failure.")
  (rest body "The forms. At least one form. Their values are dropped.")
  (see for-all with-label)
  (example """
    (for-all ((m : (OrderedMap string int)) (k : string) (v : int))
      (def m2 (orderedmap-set m k v))
      (label "after insert"
        (check (= (orderedmap-ref m2 k) v))))
    """)
  (reference """
    A failure in the body is reported as @code{in name:} and then the
    failure. When the body raises an exception, the exception is reported
    under the name as a failure, and the forms after the @code{label} still
    run. It works in any test, also outside a @code{for-all}.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Named by the templates
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc run-for-all!
  (summary "Runs a property: the stored cases first, then the random runs, then shrinking and the report.")
  (arg file "The source file of the for-all.")
  (arg line "The line of the for-all.")
  (arg runs "The number of runs that the form gives, or 0 when it gives none.")
  (arg names "The names of the bindings, in order, for the report.")
  (arg prop "A function from the choices of a run to the texts of the values and the body of the run.")
  (raises InvalidOperationException "When no test is running.")
  (see for-all)
  (reference """
    It is exported because the template of @code{for-all} names it. Write
    @code{for-all}, not a call to this function.
    """))

(:doc with-label
  (summary "Runs a function, and reports each failure in it under a name.")
  (arg name "The name that the report puts above each failure.")
  (arg body "The function.")
  (see label)
  (reference """
    It is exported because the template of @code{label} names it. Write
    @code{label}, not a call to this function.
    """))

(:doc prop-draw
  (summary "The value of a generator, drawn from the choices of a run.")
  (arg g "The generator.")
  (arg c "The choices.")
  (returns "What @code{(gen-run g c)} answers.")
  (see for-all)
  (reference """
    It is exported because the template of @code{for-all} names it, for a
    binding @code{(name gen)}.
    """))

(:doc prop-arbitrary
  (summary "The value of the Arbitrary generator of the result type, drawn from the choices of a run.")
  (arg c "The choices.")
  (returns "What @code{(gen-run (arbitrary) c)} answers. The type of the result chooses the instance.")
  (see for-all Arbitrary)
  (reference """
    It is exported because the template of @code{for-all} names it, for a
    binding @code{(name : Type)}.
    """))
